ird historical features like  that drive from the fact that it was  used starting in the 70s by banking  corporations and so for example to give  an example there's a way of storing  numbers that is I think still exists all  in SQL that is called decimal or numeric  so instead of the numbers being stored  as floating-point in binary they're  stored directly in decimal form just  like when you write them down and that's  because you absolutely don't want  rounding errors when you're dealing with  money and so when you have dollars and  cents they have to be exact  dollars in a sense and that's something  that most modern programming languages  don't support anymore but it still  exists in SQL should our users worry  about that or not fortunately the  Wolfram language has extremely good  precision tracking so it's not a problem  but in you know there's a lot of  engineering that goes into making these  two things talk together so I guess we  talked a lot about  implementation to get this to work what  are some common workflows that you see a  user using this sort of so first of all  you need to connect to so there is a  there is a symbolic representation of  the connection of the so user  name/password what the host in the port  is once you connect the first step is  inspecting the database view of what's  in there then once you're inspected the  database you only have information  that's very raw SQL and we lift that  into an entity store that can be then  registered and queried now once you have  the data in an entity store I mean the  data is not really in there it's still  in the database and it might be much  much larger than the memory with a  computer there are databases out there  with terabytes of data coming from  everything from logs of websites to  scientific data especially in biology  they have now huge droves of gene  expression and genomes and proteins and  so on so once you have the data  connected through an entity store you  can perform queries that